(verb
  :name "math/pow" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(math/pow b e) -> number"
  :summary "Computes b raised to the power e (b^e)."
  :explanation "Exponentiation is repeated multiplication: b^e means b multiplied by itself e times (when e is a positive integer). More generally, it extends to negative exponents (b^(−e) = 1/b^e), fractional exponents (b^(1/n) = nth root of b), and real exponents (via logarithms). For example, 2^3 = 8 and 4^0.5 = 2. Returns inexact floating-point values. The base b should be positive for arbitrary real exponents."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/pow 2 3)" :note "Returns 8; 2³ = 2×2×2. Basic exponentiation.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (compound-interest principal rate years)\n  (* principal (math/pow (+ 1 rate) years)))\n(compound-interest 1000 0.05 10)" :note "Finance: A = P(1 + r)^t grows exponentially at rate r over t years.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (fold-left f init xs)\n  (let loop ((xs xs) (acc init))\n    (if (null? xs) acc (loop (cdr xs) (f acc (car xs))))))\n(define (power-series x n-terms)\n  (fold-left (lambda (sum k)\n              (+ sum (/ (math/pow x k) (comb/factorial k))))\n            0\n            (list-copy (map (lambda (i) i) (list 0)))))\n(power-series 1 5)" :note "Building a power series (here sketched; e^x ≈ Σ x^k/k!). Shows composition with factorial.")
  )
  :caveats ("For negative base b and non-integer exponent e, result may be complex (not returned)." "Zero to a negative power (0^(−e)) is undefined; may escalate." "Exponent 0: b^0 = 1 for any nonzero b." "Results are inexact floating-point; precision degrades for very large or very small exponents." "1^e always returns 1 for any exponent e.")
  :drawbacks ("Floating-point precision limits; not suitable for exact symbolic work (use sym/pow)." "Cannot represent negative bases with fractional exponents." "Very large exponents can overflow to infinity; very small (negative large) exponents underflow to zero.")
  :usecases ("Modeling exponential growth (population, finance) and decay (half-life, radioactivity)." "Computing roots as fractional exponents (e.g., cube root as ^(1/3))." "Power series and polynomial evaluation." "Physics and engineering: velocity, acceleration, energy formulas.")
  :related ("math/log" "math/log-base" "math/log10" "math/log2" "math/exp" "const/e")
  :learn (:concept "Exponentiation as repeated multiplication, generalized to real exponents via logarithms." :prerequisites ("math/log") :progression "Master math/pow, then explore its inverse math/log-base, then apply both to growth/decay problems and financial modeling.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A financial model computes compound interest: A = P(1+r)^t." :programs (
      (:code "(define (compound p r t) (* p (math/pow (+ 1 r) t)))
(compound 1000 0.05 10)" :tags (program))
      (:code "(define (compound p r t) (* p (math/pow (+ 1 r) t)))\n(map (lambda (yr) (list yr (compound 1000 0.05 yr))) '(1 5 10))" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(math/pow 2 3)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (cube-root x) (math/pow x (/ 1 3)))\n(list 'cbrt-27 (cube-root 27) 'cbrt-8 (cube-root 8))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "Apply (1+r)^t and scale by principal. Eng: balance at several years. Math: 2^3 = 8. CS: fractional exponent gives roots (cube root as ^(1/3))." :meta "math/pow is the correct verb because the exponent can be any real. math/exp with log workaround is possible but less direct.")
    (:eng "Log the operator's stated problem and the code path chosen; audits catch cases where a sibling verb would silently succeed with wrong answers." :math "The audit lens fixes the mathematical function class (unary, binary, inverse, aggregate) and gates on argument-domain checks stated in the caveats." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes single-call, single-effect: pure functions may be replaced by their memoized result; audits check that no impure sibling was substituted.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: real exponentiation."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/pow 2 3)")
       (:domain "population (a metaphor domain — n doublings from an initial size)" :code "(define (pop-n n0 doublings) (list n0 doublings))")
       (:domain "math — b^e for b>0" :code "(define (power b e) (list b e))")
     )
     :explanation "Each rung raises a base to a real power."
     :meta "The shape transfers because b^e is fundamental to any scale-invariant process.")
    (:eng "Reuse of shape across substrates keeps the operator's mental model portable; the same debugging heuristics apply." :math "Cross-domain shape sharing is category-theoretic: the same universal property holds in every domain that satisfies the axioms." :cs "Cross-substrate reuse informs typeclass and trait design: the same shape becomes a shared interface across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "b^(a+c) = b^a · b^c." :programs (
      (:code "(< (abs (- (math/pow 2 5) (* (math/pow 2 2) (math/pow 2 3)))) 1e-9)" :tags (program))
      (:code "(< (abs (- (math/pow 2 5) (* (math/pow 2 2) (math/pow 2 3)))) 1e-9)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (zero-exp-is-one? b) (< (abs (- (math/pow b 0) 1)) 1e-9))\n(and (zero-exp-is-one? 2) (zero-exp-is-one? 7) (zero-exp-is-one? 0.5))" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (pow-log-inverse? b x)\n  (< (abs (- (math/log-base b (math/pow b x)) x)) 1e-9))\n(pow-log-inverse? 2 5)" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "∀ b > 0, a c ∈ ℝ. b^(a+c) = b^a · b^c" :meta "Exponent-addition law. If broken, the impl is not exponentiation. See book-of-math ch 9.")
    (:eng "If the invariant fails at runtime, that is a fault log line — the pure algebra should hold; a break implies the impl regressed." :math "The invariant is the closed-form algebraic identity that defines the verb's essence; falsification by counterexample is what makes it engineering-grade math." :cs "The runnable check is a property test in the sense of QuickCheck; make it a smoke test in CI so regressions are caught before ship.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Chain pow with a rate for a growth timeline." :programs (
      (:code "(define (grow-over n0 rate t) (* n0 (math/pow (+ 1 rate) t)))
(grow-over 100 0.1 5)" :tags (composition))
      (:code "(define (double-every n0 doublings) (* n0 (math/pow 2 doublings)))\n(map (lambda (d) (list d (double-every 1 d))) '(0 1 2 3 10))" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (powers-of b ns)\n  (map (lambda (n) (list n (math/pow b n))) ns))\n(powers-of 3 '(0 1 2 3 4))" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (bytes-for bits) (math/pow 2 bits))\n(map (lambda (b) (list 'bits b 'values (bytes-for b))) '(1 4 8 16))" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "growth-model: pow drives compound growth." :meta "Belongs to the family of exponential-growth models. See book-of-composition ch 11.")
    (:eng "Emergent patterns bring known operational discipline (retry, throttle, cache) into the verb chain; the operator inherits the pattern's diagnostics." :math "Composition preserves the algebraic properties of each verb; the emergent structure has its own (usually monoid) invariants worth naming." :cs "The named emergent (cache, throttle, sequencer, adapter, pipeline, projection) is the CS pattern-family label; reach for known-idiom debuggers when it appears.")
))

(verb
  :name "math/ratio-bar" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(math/ratio-bar a b) -> spec"
  :summary "Produces a specification for rendering a ratio bar visualizing the ratio a:b."
  :explanation "A ratio bar is a teaching model that visually represents the relationship between two quantities in a ratio a:b. The bar is divided into a units and b units (or segments proportional to a and b), helping students understand ratios as relationships between amounts. For example, a ratio 2:3 would show a bar divided into 5 equal parts, with 2 shaded on the left and 3 shaded (or unshaded) on the right. The verb returns a spec object suitable for rendering or visualization systems."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/ratio-bar 2 3)" :note "A ratio 2:3; spec describes a bar divided into 2 + 3 = 5 units.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (scale-ratio a b scale-factor)\n  (math/ratio-bar (* a scale-factor) (* b scale-factor)))\n(scale-ratio 1 4 5)" :note "Equivalent ratio: 1:4 scaled by 5 becomes 5:20. Demonstrates ratio equivalence.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (ratio-problem girls boys)\n  (let ((spec (math/ratio-bar girls boys)))\n    (list 'ratio-spec spec\n          'total (+ girls boys)\n          'girl-fraction (/ girls (+ girls boys)))))\n(ratio-problem 8 12)" :note "Word-problem context: ratio bar used to find total and fractions.")
  )
  :caveats ("a and b should both be non-negative; negative values produce undefined behavior." "If a or b is zero, the ratio is degenerate; visualizations may not render sensibly." "This returns a spec, not a rendered image; a separate verb must visualize." "Large values of a and b may be visually difficult to represent as discrete units.")
  :drawbacks ("Spec-only: requires a visualization pipeline to display the actual bar." "Not suitable for exact symbolic ratio manipulation (use for pedagogy/visualization)." "If a and b are very large, rendering may be slow or cluttered.")
  :usecases ("Teaching students to understand ratios and proportions visually." "Building interactive ratio and proportion problem solvers." "Illustrating equivalent ratios and scaling." "Creating visual aids for word problems involving mixtures, recipes, and rates.")
  :related ("math/fraction-bar" "math/number-line" "math/compare" "math/count-on" "math/area-model")
  :learn (:concept "Ratio bar: visual model showing the multiplicative relationship a:b between two quantities." :prerequisites ("math/fraction-bar") :progression "After math/ratio-bar, explore math/fraction-bar for parts of a whole, then solve/proportion for ratio equations.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A recipe-scaling lesson shows a 2:3 water-to-flour ratio." :programs (
      (:code "(define spec (math/ratio-bar 2 3))
spec" :tags (program))
      (:code "(define (recipe-bar water flour) (math/ratio-bar water flour))\n(recipe-bar 2 3)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(math/ratio-bar 1 1)" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (scaled-bar a b k) (math/ratio-bar (* a k) (* b k)))\n(list (scaled-bar 1 4 1) (scaled-bar 1 4 5))" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "Emit the spec that renders as a bar in the 2:3 proportion. Eng: water-to-flour. Math: 1:1 is an even split. CS: scale both terms to show an equivalent ratio." :meta "math/ratio-bar is the correct verb because it names the ratio pedagogy. fraction-bar shows part-of-whole; ratio-bar shows part-to-part.")
    (:eng "Log the operator's stated problem and the code path chosen; audits catch cases where a sibling verb would silently succeed with wrong answers." :math "The audit lens fixes the mathematical function class (unary, binary, inverse, aggregate) and gates on argument-domain checks stated in the caveats." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes single-call, single-effect: pure functions may be replaced by their memoized result; audits check that no impure sibling was substituted.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: part-to-part proportion."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/ratio-bar 2 3)")
       (:domain "mixing paint (a metaphor domain — 2 parts blue to 3 parts white)" :code "(define (mix a b) (list a b))")
       (:domain "math — ratio a:b as an equivalence class of pairs (na, nb)" :code "(define (proportion a b) (list a b))")
     )
     :explanation "Each rung represents a fixed ratio of two quantities."
     :meta "The shape transfers because part-to-part ratios are universal.")
    (:eng "Reuse of shape across substrates keeps the operator's mental model portable; the same debugging heuristics apply." :math "Cross-domain shape sharing is category-theoretic: the same universal property holds in every domain that satisfies the axioms." :cs "Cross-substrate reuse informs typeclass and trait design: the same shape becomes a shared interface across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "Scaling both terms of a ratio by k preserves the ratio." :programs (
      (:code "(define r1 (math/ratio-bar 2 3))
(define r2 (math/ratio-bar 4 6))
(> (length (list r1 r2)) 1)" :tags (program))
      (:code "(define r1 (math/ratio-bar 2 3))\n(define r2 (math/ratio-bar 4 6))\n(= (length (list r1 r2)) 2)" :tags (eng program))
      (:code "(define (ratio-total a b) (+ a b))\n(and (= (ratio-total 2 3) 5) (= (ratio-total 4 6) 10))" :tags (math program))
      (:code "(define (equivalent? a b c d) (= (* a d) (* b c)))\n(and (equivalent? 2 3 4 6) (not (equivalent? 2 3 4 7)))" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "∀ a b > 0, k > 0. ratio(a,b) ≡ ratio(k·a, k·b)" :meta "Scale-invariance of ratios. If broken, the visualization mismatches equivalent ratios. See book-of-math ch 6.")
    (:eng "If the invariant fails at runtime, that is a fault log line — the pure algebra should hold; a break implies the impl regressed." :math "The invariant is the closed-form algebraic identity that defines the verb's essence; falsification by counterexample is what makes it engineering-grade math." :cs "The runnable check is a property test in the sense of QuickCheck; make it a smoke test in CI so regressions are caught before ship.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Chain ratio-bar with scaling to show equivalent ratios." :programs (
      (:code "(define (equiv a b k) (list (math/ratio-bar a b) (math/ratio-bar (* a k) (* b k))))
(equiv 1 4 5)" :tags (composition))
      (:code "(define (ratio-report a b)\n  (list 'bar (math/ratio-bar a b)\n        'total (+ a b)\n        'first-share (math/pct a (+ a b))))\n(ratio-report 8 12)" :tags (eng composition))
      (:code "(define (equivalent-bars a b ks)\n  (map (lambda (k) (math/ratio-bar (* a k) (* b k))) ks))\n(equivalent-bars 2 3 '(1 2 3))" :tags (math composition))
      (:code "(define (bar-for-fraction n d)\n  (math/ratio-bar n (- d n)))\n(bar-for-fraction 3 8)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "equivalence-illustration." :meta "Belongs to the family of pedagogical parallel-views. See book-of-composition ch 15.")
    (:eng "Emergent patterns bring known operational discipline (retry, throttle, cache) into the verb chain; the operator inherits the pattern's diagnostics." :math "Composition preserves the algebraic properties of each verb; the emergent structure has its own (usually monoid) invariants worth naming." :cs "The named emergent (cache, throttle, sequencer, adapter, pipeline, projection) is the CS pattern-family label; reach for known-idiom debuggers when it appears.")
))

(verb
  :name "math/round" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(math/round n decimals) -> number"
  :summary "Round n to a given number of decimal places using half-away-from-zero rule."
  :explanation "Rounding is essential for display and comparison. This verb rounds to a fixed number of decimal places using the half-away-from-zero strategy: if the digit to be discarded is 5 or greater, round away from zero (up for positive, down for negative). For example, 2.345 rounded to 2 decimal places is 2.35, and -2.345 rounded to 2 places is -2.35. This is more predictable for teaching than banker's rounding (round-to-even)."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/round 3.14159 2)" :note "Returns 3.14 — truncated and rounded to two decimal places.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define (format-price cents)\n  (math/round (/ cents 100) 2))\n(display (format-price 1049))" :note "Convert cents to dollars and round; common in e-commerce or shoppe contexts.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (grade-with-curve raw-score scale factor)\n  (let ((adjusted (+ raw-score (* scale factor))))\n    (math/round adjusted 1)))\n(grade-with-curve 87 0.5 2)" :note "Apply a formula, then round to one decimal place for consistent display.")
  )
  :caveats ("Rounds half away from zero, not toward even (banker's rounding); -2.5 rounds to -3, not -2." "If decimals is 0, rounds to the nearest integer." "If decimals is negative, *[verify]* rounding behavior (e.g., (math/round 1234 -1) may round to nearest 10)." "Floating-point precision issues may arise with very large numbers or many decimal places.")
  :drawbacks ("Does not truncate; use (floor n) or (quotient n) if you need strict truncation." "Does not format as a string; compose with (display) or (format) for string output." "Expensive for many rounding operations in a tight loop; cache if possible.")
  :usecases ("Price formatting and currency display." "Grade reporting and score display." "Scientific or statistical output (confidence intervals, p-values)." "UI labels and dashboards requiring fixed precision.")
  :related ("math/pct" "math/round-to-place" "math/avg")
  :learn (:concept "Decimal rounding and half-away-from-zero rule." :prerequisites ("(core)/number?" "(core)/expt" "(core)/quotient") :progression "After learning math/round, study math/round-to-place to understand place-magnitude rounding (10s, 100s), then explore exact/float->rat for exact rational arithmetic.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A price display needs π rounded to 2 decimal places."
     :programs (
       (:code "(math/round 3.14159 2)" :tags (program))
       (:code "(define (price-list-round prices places)\n  (map (lambda (p) (math/round p places)) prices))\n(price-list-round (list 12.999 4.995 0.055 100.001) 2)" :tags (eng program))
       (:code "(define (round-preserves-integer? n)\n  (and (= (math/round 3.0 5) 3.0)\n       (= (math/round (- 7.0) 3) (- 7.0))))\n(round-preserves-integer? 5)" :tags (math program))
       (:code "(define (safe-round x places)\n  (cond ((not (number? x)) (error 'not-a-number))\n        ((< places 0) (error 'need-nonneg-places))\n        (else (math/round x places))))\n(safe-round 3.14159 2)" :tags (cs program)))
     :explanation "Round to 2 decimals. Eng: batch price rounding. Math: rounding integers is identity regardless of places. CS: type + precondition guards."
     :meta "math/round is the correct verb because it rounds half-away-from-zero, which is the intuitive rule. Banker's rounding would flip on 0.5 boundaries.")
    (:eng "Log the operator's stated problem and the code path chosen; audits catch cases where a sibling verb would silently succeed with wrong answers." :math "The audit lens fixes the mathematical function class (unary, binary, inverse, aggregate) and gates on argument-domain checks stated in the caveats." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes single-call, single-effect: pure functions may be replaced by their memoized result; audits check that no impure sibling was substituted.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: precision truncation with rounding."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/round 3.14159 2)")
       (:domain "supermarket prices (a metaphor domain — items priced to two decimal cents)" :code "(math/round 3.14159 2)")
       (:domain "math — approximation to a fixed precision" :code "(define (approx x p) (list x p))")
     )
     :explanation "Each rung truncates precision to a fixed number of digits."
     :meta "The shape transfers because 'to N places' is a universal presentation operation.")
    (:eng "Reuse of shape across substrates keeps the operator's mental model portable; the same debugging heuristics apply." :math "Cross-domain shape sharing is category-theoretic: the same universal property holds in every domain that satisfies the axioms." :cs "Cross-substrate reuse informs typeclass and trait design: the same shape becomes a shared interface across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "The error is bounded by half the place unit."
     :programs (
       (:code "(< (abs (- (math/round 3.14159 2) 3.14159)) 0.005)" :tags (program))
       (:code "(define (batch-error-bounded? xs)\n  (let loop ((rest xs))\n    (cond ((null? rest) #t)\n          ((> (abs (- (math/round (car rest) 3) (car rest))) 0.0005) #f)\n          (else (loop (cdr rest))))))\n(batch-error-bounded? (list 3.14159 2.71828 1.41421 0.577215))" :tags (eng program))
       (:code "(define (half-away-from-zero?)\n  (and (= (math/round 2.5 0) 3)\n       (= (math/round -2.5 0) -3)\n       (= (math/round 0.5 0) 1)))\n(half-away-from-zero?)" :tags (math program))
       (:code "(define (idempotent-at-precision? x k)\n  (= (math/round (math/round x k) k) (math/round x k)))\n(and (idempotent-at-precision? 3.14159 2)\n     (idempotent-at-precision? 7.0 5))" :tags (cs program)))
     :invariant "∀ x ∈ ℝ, k ∈ ℕ. |round(x, k) − x| ≤ 0.5·10^(-k)"
     :meta "Rounding error bound. If broken, precision guarantee is wrong. See book-of-math ch 10.")
    (:eng "If the invariant fails at runtime, that is a fault log line — the pure algebra should hold; a break implies the impl regressed." :math "The invariant is the closed-form algebraic identity that defines the verb's essence; falsification by counterexample is what makes it engineering-grade math." :cs "The runnable check is a property test in the sense of QuickCheck; make it a smoke test in CI so regressions are caught before ship.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Chain round with a formatter for currency display."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (dollar-format cents)\n  (string-append \"$\" (number->string (math/round (/ cents 100) 2))))\n(dollar-format 1049)" :tags (composition))
       (:code "(define (invoice-total-rounded items)\n  (math/round (apply + items) 2))\n(invoice-total-rounded (list 12.345 4.678 0.999 1.5))" :tags (eng composition))
       (:code "(define (fit-error-report ys y-hats)\n  (let* ((residuals (map - ys y-hats))\n         (mse (/ (apply + (map (lambda (r) (* r r)) residuals)) (length ys))))\n    (math/round (sqrt mse) 4)))\n(fit-error-report (list 1.0 2.0 3.0 4.0) (list 1.1 1.9 3.05 3.98))" :tags (math composition))
       (:code "(define (grade-with-cap raw-score cap decimals)\n  (math/round (min raw-score cap) decimals))\n(grade-with-cap 105.789 100 1)" :tags (cs composition)))
     :emergent "round-then-format. Eng: invoice total with 2-decimal round. Math: fit-error RMSE rounded to 4 places. CS: grade capped-then-rounded for display."
     :meta "Belongs to the family of presentation pipelines. See book-of-composition ch 15.")
    (:eng "Emergent patterns bring known operational discipline (retry, throttle, cache) into the verb chain; the operator inherits the pattern's diagnostics." :math "Composition preserves the algebraic properties of each verb; the emergent structure has its own (usually monoid) invariants worth naming." :cs "The named emergent (cache, throttle, sequencer, adapter, pipeline, projection) is the CS pattern-family label; reach for known-idiom debuggers when it appears.")
))

(verb
  :name "math/round-half-up" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "meta"
  :signature "(math/round-half-up x places) -> number"
  :summary "Rounds x to the specified number of decimal places using round-half-up (classical rounding)."
  :explanation "Rounding approximates a number to a simpler form with fewer decimal places. Round-half-up (also called 'commercial rounding' or 'round half away from zero') rounds .5 up: 2.5 becomes 3, 2.4 becomes 2, 2.6 becomes 3. This is the most intuitive rounding for most people. The places argument specifies the number of decimal places: places=0 rounds to an integer, places=1 rounds to one decimal place, places=−1 rounds to the nearest ten, etc. Returns an inexact floating-point number."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/round-half-up 3.7 0)" :note "Rounds 3.7 to 0 decimal places (nearest integer) = 4.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define cost 'cost)\n(define (price-from-cost markup-pct)\n  (let ((gross (* cost (+ 1 (/ markup-pct 100)))))\n    (math/round-half-up gross 2)))\n(price-from-cost 10 15)" :note "Commerce: compute price with markup, then round to 2 decimal places (cents).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (summarize-measurements measurements)\n  (let ((mean (/ (apply + measurements) (length measurements))))\n    (list 'mean-exact mean\n          'mean-rounded (math/round-half-up mean 3))))\n(summarize-measurements (list 2.3456 2.3461 2.3454))" :note "Statistics: compute exact mean, then round for reporting.")
  )
  :caveats ("places can be negative (round to tens, hundreds, etc.); places=−1 rounds to nearest 10." "Rounding .5 always rounds away from zero (up for positive, down for negative)." "Floating-point representation errors may cause subtle rounding artifacts." "This is inexact: suitable for presentation, not for exact financial or critical calculations." "Successive rounding can accumulate error; round once at the end, not at intermediate steps.")
  :drawbacks ("Does not use banker's rounding (round-half-to-even), which some contexts prefer." "Floating-point precision limits effectiveness for very precise values." "Not suitable for exact rational arithmetic (use exact/* for precise fractional results)." "Rounding .5 away from zero can introduce upward bias in large datasets.")
  :usecases ("Formatting prices and monetary values to 2 decimal places." "Rounding measurements and scientific data for presentation." "Computing averages and summaries from noisy data." "Preparing output for display in user interfaces or reports.")
  :related ("math/round" "math/exp" "math/log" "math/pow" "exact/->float" "exact/->mixed")
  :learn (:concept "Round-half-up: classical rounding where .5 rounds away from zero." :prerequisites () :progression "Use math/round-half-up for basic rounding, then explore exact/* for precise fractional arithmetic, then consider banker's rounding or truncation for specialized contexts.")
  :row-2-audit (
