(verb
  :name "math/gcd" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/gcd a b ...) -> integer"
  :arity "variadic (>=1)"
  :contract "all-integers → nonnegative integer; 0 args → 0"
  :summary "Greatest common divisor of the inputs, via Euclid's algorithm."
  :doc "Truncates each input to integer via bitwise OR 0. Result is always nonnegative. GCD of (0 0) is 0. Aliased as nt/gcd."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/gcd 12 18)" :note "Returns 6.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(math/gcd 24 36 60)" :note "Variadic — GCD of three numbers. Returns 12.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (coprime? a b) (= 1 (math/gcd a b)))\n(coprime? 14 25)" :note "Two integers are coprime iff their gcd is 1. Returns #t.")
  )
  :related ("math/lcm" "nt/gcd" "nt/mod-inverse")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A fraction reducer needs the GCD of 24 and 36 to simplify 24/36." :programs (
      (:code "(define (gcd-of a b) (math/gcd a b))
(gcd-of 24 36)" :tags (program))) :explanation "Euclid's algorithm returns 12." :meta "math/gcd is the correct verb. It's variadic and safe on integers."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (simplify num den) (let ((g (math/gcd num den))) (list (/ num g) (/ den g))))
(simplify 24 36)" :tags (eng program))
       (:code "(define (coprime? a b) (= 1 (math/gcd a b)))
(coprime? 8 15)" :tags (math program))))
    (:eng "Log the operator's stated problem and the code path chosen; audits catch cases where a sibling verb would silently succeed with wrong answers." :math "The audit lens fixes the mathematical function class (unary, binary, inverse, aggregate) and gates on argument-domain checks stated in the caveats." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes single-call, single-effect: pure functions may be replaced by their memoized result; audits check that no impure sibling was substituted.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: greatest common divisor via Euclid."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/gcd 12 18)")
       (:domain "scheduling (a metaphor domain — smallest period for two repeating tasks)" :code "(define (common-period a b) (list a b))")
       (:domain "math — the greatest generator of the ideal aℤ + bℤ" :code "(define (gcd-abstract a b) (list a b))")
     )
     :explanation "Each rung finds the common divisor of two or more values."
     :meta "The shape transfers because the GCD abstracts to any Euclidean domain.")
    (:eng "Reuse of shape across substrates keeps the operator's mental model portable; the same debugging heuristics apply." :math "Cross-domain shape sharing is category-theoretic: the same universal property holds in every domain that satisfies the axioms." :cs "Cross-substrate reuse informs typeclass and trait design: the same shape becomes a shared interface across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "gcd is symmetric." :programs (
      (:code "(= (math/gcd 12 18) (math/gcd 18 12))" :tags (program))) :invariant "∀ a b ∈ ℤ. gcd(a,b) = gcd(b,a)" :meta "Argument symmetry. If broken, the impl is order-sensitive. See book-of-math ch 3."
     :programs (
       (:code "(= (math/gcd 12 0) 12)" :tags (math program))
       (:code "(= (math/gcd 24 36 60) 12)" :tags (cs program))))
    (:eng "If the invariant fails at runtime, that is a fault log line — the pure algebra should hold; a break implies the impl regressed." :math "The invariant is the closed-form algebraic identity that defines the verb's essence; falsification by counterexample is what makes it engineering-grade math." :cs "The runnable check is a property test in the sense of QuickCheck; make it a smoke test in CI so regressions are caught before ship.")
)
