(verb
  :name "math/square" :std "Motoi"
  :library "math"
  :kind "pure"
  :dialect "motoi"
  :signature "(math/square x) -> number"
  :arity "1"
  :contract "any real → nonnegative real (for real x)"
  :summary "x squared. Equivalent to (* x x) but reads more clearly."
  :doc "Small helper to make numerical code more legible. Also useful as a first-class function to pass to map/reduce."
  :since "1.0"
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(math/square 7)" :note "Returns 49.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(map math/square '(1 2 3 4))" :note "Square each. Returns (1 4 9 16).")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define (sum-of-squares xs) (apply + (map math/square xs)))\n(sum-of-squares '(3 4 12))" :note "Common in statistics and physics. Returns 169.")
  )
  :related ("math/cube" "math/pow" "math/sqrt")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:problem "A sum-of-squares statistic needs each element squared before summing." :programs (
      (:code "(math/square 7)" :tags (program))) :explanation "Return x·x." :meta "math/square is the correct verb because it names the intent and is composable via map. Inline (* x x) works but doesn't hand to map."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (kinetic-energy m v) (* 0.5 m (math/square v)))
(kinetic-energy 2 3)" :tags (eng program))
       (:code "(define (variance-term x mean) (math/square (- x mean)))
(variance-term 7 4)" :tags (math program))))
    (:eng "Log the operator's stated problem and the code path chosen; audits catch cases where a sibling verb would silently succeed with wrong answers." :math "The audit lens fixes the mathematical function class (unary, binary, inverse, aggregate) and gates on argument-domain checks stated in the caveats." :cs "Row 2 emphasizes single-call, single-effect: pure functions may be replaced by their memoized result; audits check that no impure sibling was substituted.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Same shape: monomial x²."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "math/*" :code "(math/square 7)")
       (:domain "area of a square (a metaphor domain — side times side)" :code "(define (area s) (list s))")
       (:domain "math — x²" :code "(define (sq x) (* x x))")
     )
     :explanation "Each rung produces x² from x."
     :meta "The shape transfers because squaring is a universal polynomial atom.")
    (:eng "Reuse of shape across substrates keeps the operator's mental model portable; the same debugging heuristics apply." :math "Cross-domain shape sharing is category-theoretic: the same universal property holds in every domain that satisfies the axioms." :cs "Cross-substrate reuse informs typeclass and trait design: the same shape becomes a shared interface across implementations."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:claim "square is even: square(-x) = square(x)." :programs (
      (:code "(= (math/square -7) (math/square 7))" :tags (program))) :invariant "∀ x. square(-x) = square(x)" :meta "Even-function symmetry. If broken, the impl retains sign. See book-of-math ch 9."
     :programs (
       (:code "(>= (math/square -4) 0)" :tags (math program))
       (:code "(= (math/square 7) (* 7 7))" :tags (cs program))))
    (:eng "If the invariant fails at runtime, that is a fault log line — the pure algebra should hold; a break implies the impl regressed." :math "The invariant is the closed-form algebraic identity that defines the verb's essence; falsification by counterexample is what makes it engineering-grade math." :cs "The runnable check is a property test in the sense of QuickCheck; make it a smoke test in CI so regressions are caught before ship.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:base "Chain square with map+sum for sum-of-squares." :programs (
      (:code "(define (sos xs) (apply + (map math/square xs)))
(sos '(3 4 12))" :tags (composition))) :emergent "map-then-sum." :meta "Belongs to the family of pipeline aggregation patterns. See book-of-composition ch 7."
     :programs (
       (:code "(define (variance xs) (let ((m (/ (apply + xs) (length xs)))) (/ (apply + (map (lambda (x) (math/square (- x m))) xs)) (length xs))))
(variance '(2 4 6))" :tags (math composition))
       (:code "(define (magnitude-sq vecs) (map (lambda (v) (apply + (map math/square v))) vecs))
(magnitude-sq '((3 4) (1 2 2)))" :tags (cs composition))))
